lated', but the resulting loss of connectivity and increased latency on remaining nodes creates a feedback loop of congestion collapse, mimicking a total failure.","optional_refs":["b786fddb3d54b2be5db5cc7b11ffaa911f83a4690f0bba502facdd15b9f423a6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["b786fddb3d54b2be5db5cc7b11ffaa911f83a4690f0bba502facdd15b9f423a6"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["2b105222b09126b7207f8adc15d88b84c2cd4a8438e3321477e44a00819bba55","b786fddb3d54b2be5db5cc7b11ffaa911f83a4690f0bba502facdd15b9f423a6","beb867610f65fad792d6e61057a7c4f303ef8657c7be09c99cf8706be3e93a4a","c62db73c995919cf27a1683692493c65a28b28f16d2041898eba3dfef8e38a35"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Considering the 'Poison Pill' scenario: If a large service has 100x redundancy but a single deployment pipeline, a single bad config change is a correlated failure. If a decentralized network has 10x redundancy but 10 different deployment pipelines, the 'blast radius' of a config error is limited to 1/10th of the network. This suggests that 'operational diversity' is a more potent form of redundancy than 'hardware duplication'.","why_keep_this":"Explores the distinction between redundancy (more of the same) and diversity (different things)."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we quantify the 'complexity cost' mentioned in SCR_001 to find the exact point where adding redundancy increases the probability of a correlated failure?","related_refs":["SCR_001","SCR_002"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}